  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe core idea is to use a hybrid optimization method that combines continuous optimization with binary hill climbing. We first upsample the initial solution to 256 points and then use projected gradient descent (with a smooth softmax approximation of the maximum correlation) to refine it. Then, we convert the continuous solution to binary form and apply binary simulated annealing with local search to escape local optima. This differs from previous methods by focusing purely on continuous optimization before transitioning to binary, allowing better exploration before exploitation.\n\nMathematical specification:\nMinimize: C_5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\nSubject to:\n  0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n  \u222b h(x) dx = 1\n\nThe continuous problem is approximated using:\n  C_\u03b1(h) = (1/\u03b1) log(\u2211 exp(\u03b1 (corr_max - corr)))\n\nWith units project_onto_box_sum ensuring the constraints through iterative clipping and balancing. The binary search targets h[i] \u2208 {0, 1} with \u2211h[i] = n_points/2.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Define n_large = 256 as the target grid size\n2. If parent available, upsample using linear interpolation and project to constraints\n3. Apply smooth_grad_descent with adaptive hyperparameters\n4. Convert optimum to binary form by balancing counts\n5. Apply binary_hill_climb_sa to explore local improvements\n6. Retain best solution encountered\n\nSearch choices:\n  - Smooth optimization: max_iter=2000, step0=0.1, alpha_start=10, alpha_end=10 (fixed)\n  - Binary optimization: target=0.3808, initial_temp=0.005, beta=0.9, max_iters=10000\n  - Upsampling preserves integral section 3)\n\nNumerical robustness:\n  - Project_onto_box_sum guarantees constraints at each optimization step\n  - Finite differencing used for gradient computation as fallback\n  - Feasibility preserved through repair mechanisms\n  - Fallback candidates from lower resolutions\n\nRuntime safeguards:\n  - Time tracking via time_left callback\n  - Early termination on conservation\n  - Balanced time allocation between continuous and binary phases\n  - Graceful degradation on NaN/inf values\n\nReturn contract:\n  - (h_values, c5_bound, n_points): Grid-solution, score, and resolution (256)\n\nThe algorithmic differences from previous methods include (1) direct convex optimization for continuous relaxation, (2) explicit binary repair mechanism, and (3) hybridization of continuous and discrete phases with budget allocation.",
